      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${a}_{43}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${c}_{32}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form.",
